So what?  It's still a game.  If it's not fun it's crap!  (with apolagies
to Scotsmen everywhere...)  What's so cool about instant, pointless,
choiceless, impossible-to-do-or-say-anything-about, death?   I mean, why not
just have random traps that say, "You don't have disintegration resistance,
you die!", or "Not cold resistent?  Tough luck, schmuck....(picture of
gravestone)"

And it's a fallacy to say that you need instant death to kill a tough
charater.  I've died of poison lots of times (or had it be a crucial factor
in my death) without it being an instant death.  Poison can be irritating
enough without instant death.  (How's that for a great quote?  :-) )

One other thing, as long we're talking about *real* poisons (Neurotoxins,
indeed...what sort of a self-respecting dark-ages alchemist would be
dabbling in neurotoxins?!?!  Anyone knows they're against the Geneva
convention!), it's not like you can develop a poison immunity to hemlock
and have it help you against *neurotoxins*.  I don't think there *is*
a such thing as immunity to neurotoxins.  Bleh!

